* sync.Mutex

We've seen how channels are great for communication among goroutines.

But what if we don't need communication? What if we just want to make sure only
one ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000001⟫ can access a variable at a time to avoid conflicts?

This concept is called ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000002⟫mutual_exclusion⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000003⟫, and the conventional name for the data structure that provides it is ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000004⟫⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000005⟫⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000006⟫.

⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000007⟫'s standard library provides mutual exclusion with
[[⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000008⟫][⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000009⟫sync.Mutex⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000010⟫]] and its two methods:

- ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000011⟫Lock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000012⟫
- ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000013⟫Unlock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000014⟫

We can define a block of code to be executed in mutual exclusion by surrounding it
with a call to ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000015⟫Lock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000016⟫ and ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000017⟫Unlock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000018⟫ as shown on the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000019⟫Inc⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000020⟫ method.

We can also use ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000021⟫defer⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000022⟫ to ensure the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000023⟫ will be unlocked as in the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000024⟫Value⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000025⟫ method.

